>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Problems · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Battery Terminal Corrosion: The Crust That Stops Starting</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        The white-blue crust on a battery terminal is not dirt — it is a chemical reaction escaping from inside
        the battery, and it explains a surprising share of "dead battery" diagnoses.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Terminal corrosion is the <strong>white-blue crust</strong> of lead sulfate
        and oxides that forms when <strong>acid vapour and hydrogen</strong> escape around the terminal seal during
        charging and react with the lead terminal in air. The crust <strong>raises resistance</strong>, blocks
        charging current and eventually causes starting failures — and it often signals overcharging underneath.
      </div>

      <h2>Cause → Mechanism → Symptoms</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Cause</strong></td><td>Acid vapour/hydrogen escaping the terminal seal — worse with overcharge</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Mechanism</strong></td><td>Vapour reacts with lead + air → lead sulfate/oxide crust</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Symptoms</strong></td><td>Crust on terminals, slow cranking, no-start, weak charging</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Diagnosis and prevention</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Diagnosis:</strong> the crust itself is the diagnosis — check the charging system too, because
        corrosion that keeps returning points to overcharging or a leaking seal, not just age.
        <strong>Prevention:</strong> clean with baking soda and water (neutralises the acid), rinse and dry, then
        protect the connection with terminal grease. The fix lasts only if the vapour source is addressed
        (see <a href="/lead-acid/agm-charging/">charging discipline</a>).
      </p>
      <h2>When corrosion means something deeper</h2>
      <p>
        Heavy, recurring corrosion is often a symptom, not just a mess: overcharging accelerates gassing, and the
        acid vapour drives the corrosion — so a battery that keeps crusting over may be telling you its charging
        voltage sits too high (see <a href="/lead-acid/agm-charging/">charging limits</a>). Clean it, but also
        ask why it keeps coming back.
      </p>


      <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>I would argue:</strong> Terminal corrosion is the best-known battery problem and the most misread one —
          it is usually a symptom of overcharging or a failing seal, not a cosmetic blemish, so cleaning it without
          finding the source just schedules the next no-start.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>What drives this:</strong> The crust is the reaction's visible half; the invisible half is the vapour still
          escaping. Ten minutes of cleaning removes the symptom; five minutes of checking the charging voltage
          removes the cause — and the second is the repair that actually lasts.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Why do battery terminals corrode?</h3>
      <p>Acid vapour and hydrogen escape around the terminal seal during charging, then react with the lead terminal and air to form the white-blue crust — lead sulfate and oxides. Overcharging accelerates the vapour, so corrosion often signals a charging problem.</p>
      <h3>How do you fix corroded battery terminals?</h3>
      <p>Disconnect, clean the crust with a brush and a baking-soda-and-water solution (neutralises acid), rinse, dry, and protect with terminal grease. The cause — acid vapour — should also be checked, since corrosion returns if overcharging continues.</p>
<h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Is the corrosion from acid creep or overcharging, and is the underlying cause being fixed?</li>
  <li>Are the terminals cleaned, tightened and protected after diagnosis, not just wiped?</li>
  <li>Does recurring corrosion signal a deeper fault — overcharge, a leak, or a bad ground?</li>
  <li>Will a terminal protector or felt washer prevent the recurrence?</li>
</ul>
<h2>The bottom line</h2>
<p>Terminal corrosion is a symptom as often as a nuisance — clean and protect the terminal, but find the cause, because recurring crust usually means overcharge, a leak or a bad connection. Fix the cause or the clean terminal corrodes again.</p>

